course: dictionary
lesson: 4636
title: Hinduism
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Hinduism is a noun. It names a religion, and it is the main religion of India and Nepal. With a capital H, the word names the religion itself, and «Hindu» describes the people and things connected with it.
explain: Context gives you clues. «Hinduism began in India and is one of the oldest religions in the world.» The clues are India and oldest religions, so Hinduism must be a very old religion that started in India. Swap your meaning in: "a very old religion that started in India" still makes sense.
explain: Hinduism is more than one idea. It includes gods such as Vishnu, Shiva and Lakshmi, and ideas like karma and dharma (duty). Hindus worship at a mandir (temple) and at home. «Hindu festivals» such as Diwali and Holi are part of the same way of life.
fact: Hinduism is the main religion of India and Nepal.
fact: Hinduism is one of the oldest religions in the world, and it began in India.
fact: Hindus worship one supreme reality, Brahman, often through gods and goddesses such as Vishnu, Shiva and Lakshmi.
fact: A mandir is a temple, where Hindus go to worship.
fact: Hindus celebrate festivals such as Diwali and Holi, and they read holy texts such as the Vedas and the Bhagavad Gita.
exercise: «Hinduism began in India and is one of the oldest religions in the world.» Where did Hinduism begin? | Australia | The UK | Nepal | *India
exercise: Hindus worship at a mandir. What is a mandir? | A type of food | *A temple | A holy book | A school
exercise: «Hinduism has many gods and goddesses, and many Hindus see them as forms of one ultimate reality.» What does this suggest? | Hinduism has no gods at all | Hindus worship only in one building | *Many gods can be seen as forms of one reality | Only goddesses are worshipped
test: Which is the main religion of India and Nepal? | *Hinduism | Sikhism | Buddhism | Judaism
test: «Hindus celebrate Diwali and Holi.» What are Diwali and Holi? | Holy books | Temples | Hindu gods | *Hindu festivals
exam: Which word means the one supreme reality that many Hindus see the gods as forms of? | *Brahman | Mandir | Karma | Dharma
